THOUGHT: The NullAway error is because `longAdapter.read(in)` returns a `@Nullable Number`, so `value.longValue()` may dereference null. However, this `TypeAdapter<AtomicLong>` is wrapped with `.nullSafe()`, which guarantees that `read` is never called when the JSON value is `null`; instead, Gson’s `nullSafe` wrapper handles nulls and never passes them through. To make this clear to NullAway without changing runtime behavior, I can add an explicit null check that throws if `value` is null (which should be unreachable in practice). I’ll update the method accordingly and then run the compile task to ensure the error count drops by one and no new errors are introduced.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/gson && sed -i '432,435s/AtomicLong read(JsonReader in) throws IOException {
        Number value = longAdapter.read(in);
        return new AtomicLong(value.longValue());
      }/AtomicLong read(JsonReader in) throws IOException {
        Number value = longAdapter.read(in);
        if (value == null) {
          throw new IllegalStateException("AtomicLong value must not be null");
        }
        return new AtomicLong(value.longValue());
      }/' gson/src/main/java/com/google/gson/Gson.java && ./gradlew :gson:compileJava -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 && grep -n "Gson.java:434" new_build_output.log || tail -n 40 new_build_output.log```

